}

Progress status for this period: insufficient_data. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "No strength data was recorded this period, so keep logging lifts to track your muscle-building progress.",
  "summary": "You put in some work this week across chest, back, and legs, totalling 12 sets and 5,520 kg of tonnage, which is a reasonable starting point for a muscle-building goal at your profile. However, because no strength index data was captured, it's impossible to assess whether your lifts are progressing, stalling, or regressing. The volume split looks fairly balanced across the three muscle groups, which is a positive sign. The priority right now is making sure your workouts are being logged with enough detail to track strength over time.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "The strength index data is completely empty this period, which means progressive overload cannot be assessed. The progress status is therefore marked as insufficient_data — there are no index values to compare against a previous period or against the baseline of 100 used for first-time recordings. This is not necessarily a sign of poor training, but it does mean there is no evidence yet of upward progression. To unlock this analysis in future periods, every working set should be logged with the weight used and reps completed so that a meaningful strength index can be calculated.",
    "status": "insufficient_data"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs account for 29% of total volume this period, noticeably lower than chest (34.8%) and back (36.2%). For a muscle-building goal, legs are a large muscle group that typically benefits from volume on par with or exceeding upper-body groups. This gap is mild at this stage but worth monitoring."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Start logging every set with the exact weight and reps completed — this is the single most important step to enable strength tracking and progressive overload analysis next period.",
    "Gradually shift one or two sets per session toward legs to bring leg volume closer to 33-35% of total, keeping your three-group split more balanced for overall muscle development.",
    "Apply a simple progression rule to your main lifts: when you can complete all target reps with good form, add a small increment of weight in the next session to ensure ongoing overload.",
    "Maintain your current training frequency and total set count of around 12 sets as a foundation, and aim to build toward 15-18 sets per week as your recovery allows, in line with your muscle-building goal."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Twelve sets logged across chest, back, and legs with a broadly balanced split, but no strength index data was available, making progressive overload assessment impossible — logging weights and reps every session is the top priority going into next period."
}
